messages:
Hana Lee: Quick weird one from a Windows-based customer. Their Electron app streams SSE from our hosted API and some events never surface. Turns out their platform emits CRLF (\r\n) line endings and our example parser only splits on \n\n. When a chunk boundary splits the CR and LF across two chunks we miss the delimiter and hang on a partial frame.

Marco Varela: interesting. so chunk A ends with '\r' and chunk B starts with '\n\r\n' and our split-by-\n\n logic misses the boundary. That would make the leftover buffer keep growing until an unrelated delimiter arrives.

Tess Nguyen: yup. I saw a trace: server sent comment lines (SSE comments starting with ':') and then event payload. The combination of CRLF and an intermediate chunk boundary resulted in us treating the comment + payload as one run-on frame.

Hana Lee: repro gist I used (Electron fetch):
```js
const res = await fetch('/v1/sse');
const reader = res.body.getReader();
const decoder = new TextDecoder();
let buf = '';
while(true){
  const {done, value} = await reader.read();
  if(done) break;
  buf += decoder.decode(value, {stream:true});
  // naive split
  const parts = buf.split('\n\n');
  buf = parts.pop();
  for(const p of parts){
    // handle p
  }
}
```
This fails when CRLF is used and the CR/LF pair is split across reads.

Marco Varela: concrete fixes: normalize line endings before splitting (replace all CRLF with LF) and always use streaming TextDecoder. Also treat SSE comments (lines starting with ':') specially rather than merging into JSON frames.

Tess Nguyen: SDK change proposal:
- In browser/Electron SDK, when assembling text from chunks, run `text = text.replace(/\r\n/g, '\n')` before splitting on `\n\n`.
- Handle SSE comment lines: if a block starts with ':' treat it as metadata (e.g., resume token or commentary) and emit as `comment` event instead of trying to JSON.parse.
- On stream close with leftover bytes, emit `stream_incomplete` diagnostic with base64 dump and a short hint (e.g., `possible_crlf_split`).

Hana Lee: also saw a customer that had a proxy which inserted a `:heartbeat` comment every 15s. Our parser appended those into the same frame if the CRLF split occurred, producing invalid JSON. Normalizing CRLF fixes it, but we should also prefer line-by-line parsing instead of whole-block split when possible.

Marco Varela: parity note: Node SDK already normalizes `\r\n` and supports comment events. Browser sample app missed it. We should update quickstart and add a tiny electron sample under `devex/sample-apps/electron` showing correct handling.

Tess Nguyen: another nuance: when using TextEncoder/TextDecoder, be careful to call `decoder.decode(value, {stream:true})` per chunk and then `decoder.decode()` with no stream at the end to flush. Otherwise split multibyte sequences (emoji) and CRLF pairs break.

Hana Lee: retry semantics: if a comment indicates resume token (e.g. `: resume: <token>`), SDK should save last token and expose `lastEventId` in the stream object. Reconnect attempts can pass `Last-Event-ID` header to server. But default: do not auto-retry if any event body has been emitted. Resume must be opt-in.

Marco Varela: docs & tests: add unit tests for CRLF split across chunk boundary, SSE comment handling, and proxy heartbeat comment merging. Also add integration test for Electron backgrounding + CRLF semantics.

devex-bot: Created ticket INF-2247 "electron-sse: CRLF split + comment merging causes dropped events" and assigned Hana, Marco, Tess.

Hana Lee: temporary workaround for affected customers: normalize incoming data early: `const s = decoder.decode(chunk, {stream:true}).replace(/\r\n/g,'\n')` and buffer until `\n\n`. That fixed their production case.

Tess Nguyen: I'll draft the SDK patch to normalize CRLF and emit `comment` events first thing tomorrow. Marco, can you add the electron sample?

Marco Varela: on it. I'll also add a small note in the onboarding guide about CRLF vs LF and proxies that insert heartbeats.

Hana Lee: thanks all. once PRs link to INF-2247 ping QA so they can validate on the customer's app.
